  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the functions $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ the excluded values are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0.$ Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ so it is never equal to zero. Therefore, this equation has no solutions.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={45}^{∘},\\,{225}^{∘}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={45}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={225}^{∘}.\n$$",
